WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.060
People who have been fucked by the system
who are now trying ways to unfuck themselves.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.880
There’s a lot of things that people think
is like 15 years away

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.480
that you’ll see in six to eight months.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.300
We’re in a world of exponentially advancing
technology.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:17.880
And exponentially advancing technology cannot
survive the bottlenecks of bureaucracy.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:23.580
Here in this downtown Oakland community center,

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:28.300
over 100 citizen scientists gathered for the
BioHack the Planet conference.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:33.000
Many of them have set up labs in garages and
don’t have any formal degree whatsoever.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.940
The stigma of smart people and science is
invented.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:40.140
Science works no matter who you are.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.200
Instead of breaking into and manipulating
computer systems like typical hackers,

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:48.120
they’re focused on hacking living organisms
with the hopes of curing illness

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.399
and in some cases obtaining superhuman powers.

00:00:51.399 --> 00:00:56.269
Their shared mission is to put this technology
into the hands of as many people as possible.

00:00:56.269 --> 00:01:00.600
People should be able to use all the technologies
that science develops.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:01.200
Right?

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.800
It shouldn’t just be patented and given
to companies

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.980
or exclusively given to certain people.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:10.140
There’s a lot of innovation that people
have no idea about.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:11.900
And if you’re not way up in the middle of
it

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:14.720
there’s a lot of things that people think
is like 15 years away

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.980
that you’ll see in six to eight months.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.520
And I mean it’s crazy.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.780
These do-it-yourself biologists say that the
democratization of science

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:27.720
has given them the freedom to work on projects
that are often ignored by larger institutions.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.040
They’re using gene editing technologies
like CRISPR

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:34.300
to create personalized treatments for those
suffering from rare diseases or cancer,

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:38.780
experimenting with magnetic and vibrating
implants to alter the sensory experience,

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.220
reverse engineering pharmaceuticals like Epi-Pens

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:43.810
so people can make their own medicine at home,

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:46.440
and even creating glow in the dark beer.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:53.380
I think this is the most exciting time thus
far in the history of the world to be alive.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:57.080
Personalized medicine is going to change everything.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:02.200
It’s going to rock the world because you
can’t do a clinical trial on one person.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.760
But medicine is starting to progress to the
point where I can sequence your genome,

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:07.580
I can sequence your cancer’s genome.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:08.640
I can find the difference.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:12.800
I can develop a immunotherapy so that I can
get your body to respond to your cancer specifically—

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.580
not anybody else’s cancer—

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.020
but I also can’t test that on anybody but
you.

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:20.800
That’s going to be crazy and that's going
to be awesome

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.170
Earlier this year the Food and Drug Administration

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:27.460
began placing restrictions on non-human genetic
modifications

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:32.340
declared that genetically edited animals
must be classified as drugs.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:37.079
This would give the agency broad authority
over a number of do-it-yourself genetics tests

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.480
and would require experiments involving animals

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.480
to go through the same vetting process as a new drug.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.180
I guess they couldn’t call them cosmetics
and they couldn’t call them foods

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:46.640
so they’re like dogs are drugs.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.300
David Ishee, a canine breeder from Mississippi

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:52.540
who is working on editing out genetic diseases
in dogs,

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:57.460
says the restrictions will mean only large
companies can research genetic cures for animals.

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:01.360
Everybody’s worried about what somebody
could do with this technology

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.140
and nobody seems to care about the damage
that not doing it will cause.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:09.440
Like these animals that are dying are dying
anyway.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.120
Like they’re going to die every day until
we do something about it.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:16.460
Regulation could undermine biohacking breakthroughs
for humans as well.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:21.080
And people are so afraid of how will this
be used in the wrong way.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:22.760
You know what I’m afraid of?

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:25.520
I’m afraid that all these people dying of
fucking cancer

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:29.060
and all these people who are dying of hunger

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:32.700
aren’t going to get cured, aren’t going
to get fed

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:36.760
because we’re not allowing people to have
access to this technology.

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:42.219
And it’s governed by a regulatory framework
cobbled together in the mid-80s

00:03:42.219 --> 00:03:48.849
out of statutes that are now 70 and 80 years
old that existed before we knew DNA was important.

00:03:48.849 --> 00:03:51.090
The regulations don’t fit the reality

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:57.160
and are not set up to try to distinguish between
good uses and bad uses.

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:00.260
I’m a huge fan of deregulation

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:06.100
because I believe in the inherent goodness
of like capitalism and everything.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:12.940
Like stuff doesn’t progress unless people
do useful things with it, right?

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:17.260
And people are naturally inclined to stop
bad things that happen with it.

00:04:17.269 --> 00:04:20.229
We will be able to design life

00:04:20.229 --> 00:04:24.360
in ways that the 19th century taught us how
to design buildings.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:30.220
Life is going to become something that we
make and remake and edit and reedit.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:32.190
If we are genuine proponents of freedom,

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:38.860
then people should have the liberty to use
their bodies in whatever way they see fit

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:44.600
and to undertake inquiry and think and explore
however they see fit.

